<section class="col-xs-12 col-sm-12 col-md-12 col-lg-9 col-xl-9" id="main">
<div class="html" id="tag">
<h3 class="tex-subsection" id="subsection:orthogonality"><span data-tag="04NJ">9.6.6</span> Orthogonality</h3>
<p>
We now study variant of Definition <a data-tag="04MY" href="/tag/04MY">9.6.4.8</a>. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="04NN">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04NN">Definition <span data-tag="04NN">9.6.6.1</span> <span class="named">(Orthogonality)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, and let $f: A \rightarrow B$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$ be morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. We say that $f$ is <em>left orthogonal</em> to $g$ if the composition </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^1 \xrightarrow { f \times g} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \times \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\xrightarrow { \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(\bullet ,\bullet ) } \operatorname{\mathcal{S}} \]
</div>
<p> determines a pullback diagram </p>
<div class="equation" id="06W4">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="06W4" href="/tag/06W4">9.32</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:diagram-describing-orthogonality} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(B,X) \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X) \ar [d] \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( B, Y) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A, Y) } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p> in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$. In this case, we also say that $g$ is <em>right orthogonal</em> to $f$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="06W5">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06W5">Remark <span data-tag="06W5">9.6.6.2</span>.</a> In the formulation of Definition <a data-tag="04NN" href="/tag/04NN">9.6.6.1</a>, we have implicitly assumed that the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally small, so that the $\operatorname{Hom}$-functor </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(\bullet , \bullet ): \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \times \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}} \]
</div>
<p> is well-defined (see §<a data-tag="03MR" href="/tag/03MR">8.3.3</a>). More generally, we say that $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$ if the diagram (<a data-tag="06W4" href="/tag/06W4">9.32</a>) is a pullback square in $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa }$, where $\kappa $ is any uncountable cardinal for which $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally $\kappa $-small. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="04NQ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04NQ">Remark <span data-tag="04NQ">9.6.6.3</span> <span class="named">(Symmetry)</span>.</a> Let $f$ and $g$ be morphisms in an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ if and only if it is right orthogonal to $g$ when regarded as a morphism of the opposite $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-variant" id="04NT">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04NT">Variant <span data-tag="04NT">9.6.6.4</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $S$ and $T$ be collections of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. We say that <em>$S$ is left orthogonal to $T$</em> if every morphism $f \in S$ is left orthogonal to every morphism $g \in T$. In this case, we also say that <em>$T$ is right orthogonal to $S$</em>. In the special case where $S = \{ f\} $ is a singleton, we abbreviate this condition by saying that <em>$f$ is left orthogonal to $T$</em>, or <em>$T$ is right orthogonal to $f$</em>. In the special case $T = \{ g\} $ is a singleton, we abbreviate this condition by saying that <em>$g$ is right orthogonal to $S$</em>, or <em>$S$ is left orthogonal to $g$</em>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="06W6">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06W6">Example <span data-tag="06W6">9.6.6.5</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a locally Kan simplicial category containing morphisms $f: A \rightarrow B$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$. Then $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{hc}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ if and only if the diagram of Kan complexes </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(B,X)_{\bullet } \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X)_{\bullet } \ar [d] \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( B, Y)_{\bullet } \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A, Y)_{\bullet } } \]
</div>
<p> is a homotopy pullback square. See Proposition <a data-tag="03N7" href="/tag/03N7">8.3.6.2</a> and Example <a data-tag="03G5" href="/tag/03G5">7.6.3.2</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="06W7">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06W7">Example <span data-tag="06W7">9.6.6.6</span>.</a> Let $f: A \rightarrow B$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$ be morphisms of Kan complexes. Suppose that there exists an integer $n$ such that $f$ is $n$-connective and $g$ is $(n-1)$-truncated. Then $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$. This follows from Corollary <a data-tag="056U" href="/tag/056U">3.5.9.32</a> and Example <a data-tag="06W6" href="/tag/06W6">9.6.6.5</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-variant" id="06W8">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06W8">Variant <span data-tag="06W8">9.6.6.7</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{B}}$ and $G: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be functors between (small) $\infty $-categories, where $F$ is categorically $n$-connective and $G$ is $n$-faithful. Then $F$ is left orthogonal to $G$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. This follows from Corollary <a data-tag="05CH" href="/tag/05CH">4.8.5.23</a> (together with Example <a data-tag="06W6" href="/tag/06W6">9.6.6.5</a> and Proposition <a data-tag="033C" href="/tag/033C">4.5.3.15</a>). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="06W9">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06W9">Example <span data-tag="06W9">9.6.6.8</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{B}}$ and $G: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be cocontinuous functors between presentable $\infty $-categories, which we regard as morphisms in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}^{\operatorname{LPr}}$ of Construction <a data-tag="06PF" href="/tag/06PF">9.5.3.1</a>. If $F$ is a Bousfield localization functor and $G$ is conservative, then $F$ is left orthogonal to $G$. According to Example <a data-tag="06W6" href="/tag/06W6">9.6.6.5</a>, this is equivalent to the assertion that the diagram of Kan complexes </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{LFun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{B}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq } \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{LFun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{A}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq } \ar [d] \\ \operatorname{LFun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{B}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq } \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{LFun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{A}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq } } \]
</div>
<p> is a homotopy pullback square. By virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="06VL" href="/tag/06VL">9.5.7.5</a>, we may assume without loss of generality that there is a small collection $W$ of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ such that $\operatorname{\mathcal{B}}$ is the full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{A}}$ spanned by the $W$-local objects and that $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{B}}$ is left adjoint to the inclusion functor $\operatorname{\mathcal{B}}\hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{A}}$. The desired result now follows from the universal property of $\operatorname{\mathcal{B}}$ supplied by Proposition <a data-tag="06VJ" href="/tag/06VJ">9.5.7.2</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="06WA">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WA">Remark <span data-tag="06WA">9.6.6.9</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing morphisms $f: A \rightarrow B$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$. If either $f$ or $g$ is an isomorphism, then $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$. See Corollary <a data-tag="048F" href="/tag/048F">7.6.2.39</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="06WB">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WB">Proposition <span data-tag="06WB">9.6.6.10</span> <span class="named">(Transitivity)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing a morphism $g: X \rightarrow Y$ and a $2$-simplex </p>
<div class="equation" id="04P1">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="04P1" href="/tag/04P1">9.33</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:two-out-of-three-for-orthogonal} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ &amp;  B \ar [dr]^{ f'' } &amp;  \\ A \ar [ur]^{f'} \ar [rr]^{f} &amp;  &amp;  C. } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p> Assume that $f'$ is left orthogonal to $g$. Then $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$ if and only if $f''$ is left orthogonal to $g$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have a commutative diagram of morphism spaces 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X) \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(B,X) \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X) \ar [d] \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C, Y) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( B, Y) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A, Y) } \]
</div>
<p>
       where the right square is a pullback. Applying Proposition <a data-tag="03FZ" href="/tag/03FZ">7.6.2.40</a>, we see that the left square is a pullback if and only if the outer rectangle is a pullback. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
<p>
<article class="env-warning" id="04P2">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04P2">Warning <span data-tag="04P2">9.6.6.11</span>.</a> In the situation of Proposition <a data-tag="06WB" href="/tag/06WB">9.6.6.10</a>, if the morphisms $f$ and $f''$ are left orthogonal to $g$, then $f'$ need not be left orthogonal to $g$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="06WC">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WC">Proposition <span data-tag="06WC">9.6.6.12</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing a morphism $g: X \rightarrow Y$. Then the collection of morphisms which are left orthogonal to $g$ is closed under levelwise colimits in $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      For every morphism $f: A \rightarrow B$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, let $\sigma _{f}$ denote the diagram of morphism spaces 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(B,X) \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X) \ar [d] \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( B, Y) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A, Y) } \]
</div>
<p>
       appearing in Definition <a data-tag="04NN" href="/tag/04NN">9.6.6.1</a>. Using Proposition <a data-tag="039L" href="/tag/039L">7.4.1.22</a>, we see that the construction $f \mapsto \sigma _{f}$ carries (levelwise) colimit diagrams in $\operatorname{Fun}(\Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ to limit diagrams in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{S}})$. Applying Corollary <a data-tag="06B0" href="/tag/06B0">7.3.8.6</a>, we see that the collection of those morphisms $f$ such that $\sigma _{f}$ is a pullback diagram is closed under levelwise colimits in $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="04P3">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04P3">Corollary <span data-tag="04P3">9.6.6.13</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $g: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. If a morphism $f: A \rightarrow B$ is left orthogonal to $g$, then any retract of $f$ (in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}(\Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$) is also left orthogonal to $g$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Combine Proposition <a data-tag="06WC" href="/tag/06WC">9.6.6.12</a> with Remark <a data-tag="0401" href="/tag/0401">8.5.3.9</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="04P4">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04P4">Corollary <span data-tag="04P4">9.6.6.14</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing a pushout diagram </p>
<div class="equation" id="04P5">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="04P5" href="/tag/04P5">9.34</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:pushout-of-left-orthogonal} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ A \ar [r] \ar [d]^{f} &amp;  A' \ar [d]^{f'} \\ B \ar [r] &amp;  B'. } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p> If a morphism $g: X \rightarrow Y$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is right orthogonal to $f$, then it is also right orthogonal to $f'$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      The diagram (<a data-tag="04P5" href="/tag/04P5">9.34</a>) determines a levelwise pushout square 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{id}_{A} \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{id}_{A'} \ar [d] \\ f \ar [r] &amp;  f' } \]
</div>
<p>
       in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. Since $g$ is right orthogonal to $f$, $\operatorname{id}_{A}$, and $\operatorname{id}_{A'}$ (Remark <a data-tag="06WA" href="/tag/06WA">9.6.6.9</a>), it is also right orthogonal to $f'$ (Proposition <a data-tag="06WC" href="/tag/06WC">9.6.6.12</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="06WD">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WD">Corollary <span data-tag="06WD">9.6.6.15</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $f: A \rightarrow B$ be a morphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which admits a relative codiagonal $\gamma _{A/B}: B \coprod _{A} B \rightarrow B$ (see Variant <a data-tag="04K7" href="/tag/04K7">7.6.2.23</a>). If $g$ is a morphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which is right orthogonal to $f$, then it is right orthogonal to $\gamma _{A/B}$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have a commutative diagram 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ &amp;  B \coprod _{A} B \ar [dr]^{\gamma _{A/B}} &amp;  \\ B \ar [ur]^{f'} \ar [rr]^{\operatorname{id}_ B} &amp;  &amp;  B, } \]
</div>
<p>
       where $f'$ is a pushout of $f$ and therefore left orthogonal to $g$ (Corollary <a data-tag="04P4" href="/tag/04P4">9.6.6.14</a>). Since $\operatorname{id}_{B}$ is automatically left orthogonal to $g$ (Remark <a data-tag="06WA" href="/tag/06WA">9.6.6.9</a>), it follows that $\gamma _{A/B}$ is also left orthogonal to $g$ (Proposition <a data-tag="06WB" href="/tag/06WB">9.6.6.10</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
<p>
We now connect the concept of orthogonality to the notion of lifting problem studied in §<a data-tag="04MH" href="/tag/04MH">9.6.4</a>. For this, it will be convenient to have a more concrete model for the commutative diagram which appears in the formulation of Definition <a data-tag="04NN" href="/tag/04NN">9.6.6.1</a>. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-notation" id="06WE">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WE">Notation <span data-tag="06WE">9.6.6.16</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. For every sequence of objects $C_0, C_1, \cdots , C_ n \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, let $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( C_0, C_1, \cdots , C_ n )$ be the Kan complex introduced in Notation <a data-tag="01PG" href="/tag/01PG">4.6.8.1</a>. If $f: A \rightarrow B$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$ are morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, we define simplicial sets </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,Y) = \{ f\}  \times _{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B) } \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,Y)  \]
</div>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X,Y)_{g} = \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X,Y) \times _{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y) } \{ g\}  \]
</div>
<div class="equation">
  \[  _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} = \{ f\}  \times _{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B) } \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y) \times _{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y) } \{ g\} . \]
</div>
<p> Note that Proposition <a data-tag="01PK" href="/tag/01PK">4.6.8.4</a> supplies trivial Kan fibrations </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,Y) \ar [d] &amp;  _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X,Y)_{g} \ar [d] \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(B,Y) &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( B,X ) &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X). } \]
</div>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="06WF">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WF">Remark <span data-tag="06WF">9.6.6.17</span>.</a> In the situation of Notation <a data-tag="06WE" href="/tag/06WE">9.6.6.16</a>, the fiber product </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,Y) \times _{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,Y) } \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X,Y)_{g} \]
</div>
<p> is isomorphic to the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, g)$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="06WG">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WG">Proposition <span data-tag="06WG">9.6.6.18</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing morphisms $f: A \rightarrow B$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$. Then the (strictly commutative) diagram of Kan complexes </p>
<div class="equation" id="06WH">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="06WH" href="/tag/06WH">9.35</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:diagram-describing-orthogonality-concrete} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X,Y)_{g} \ar [d] \\ _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,Y) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,Y) } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p> determines a diagram $\Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^1 \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$, which is isomorphic to the diagram (<a data-tag="06W4" href="/tag/06W4">9.32</a>) appearing in Definition <a data-tag="04NN" href="/tag/04NN">9.6.6.1</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
We will give the proof of Proposition <a data-tag="06WG" href="/tag/06WG">9.6.6.18</a> at the end of this section. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-theorem" id="06WJ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WJ">Theorem <span data-tag="06WJ">9.6.6.19</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing morphisms $f: A \rightarrow B$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The diagram of Kan complexes 
</p>
<div class="equation" id="06WK">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="06WK" href="/tag/06WK">9.36</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:orthogonality-made-concrete} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X,Y)_{g} \ar [d] \\ _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,Y) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,Y) } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p>
 is a homotopy pullback square. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The diagram (<a data-tag="06WK" href="/tag/06WK">9.36</a>) induces a homotopy equivalence $\theta : _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, g)$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(4)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The map $\theta $ is a trivial Kan fibration. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(5)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every lifting problem $\sigma :$ 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ A \ar [d]^{f} \ar [r] &amp;  X \ar [d]^{g} \\ B \ar [r] \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] &amp;  Y } \]
</div>
<p>
 in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the solution space $\operatorname{Sol}( \sigma )$ of Construction <a data-tag="04NK" href="/tag/04NK">9.6.4.17</a> is a contractible Kan complex. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
Stated more informally, Theorem <a data-tag="06WJ" href="/tag/06WJ">9.6.6.19</a> asserts that $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$ if and only if every lifting problem 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ A \ar [d]^{f} \ar [r] &amp;  X \ar [d]^{g} \\ B \ar [r] \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] &amp;  Y } \]
</div>
<p>
 has an essentially unique solution. 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof of Theorem <a data-tag="06WJ" href="/tag/06WJ">9.6.6.19</a>.</strong>
       The equivalence $(1) \Leftrightarrow (2)$ follows from Proposition <a data-tag="06WG" href="/tag/06WG">9.6.6.18</a> (and Example <a data-tag="03G5" href="/tag/03G5">7.6.3.2</a>). It follows from Corollary <a data-tag="01F3" href="/tag/01F3">4.4.5.3</a> that the restriction maps 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X,Y)_{g} \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,Y) \quad \quad _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}(\Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( f, g) \]
</div>
<p>
       are isofibrations between Kan complexes, and therefore Kan fibrations (Corollary <a data-tag="01NW" href="/tag/01NW">4.4.3.10</a>). The equivalences $(2) \Leftrightarrow (3)$ and $(3) \Leftrightarrow (4) \Leftrightarrow (5)$ now follow from Propositions <a data-tag="010N" href="/tag/010N">3.4.1.2</a> and <a data-tag="00X1" href="/tag/00X1">3.2.7.2</a>, respectively. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="04NP">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04NP">Example <span data-tag="04NP">9.6.6.20</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an ordinary category. Then a morphism $f: A \rightarrow B$ is left orthogonal to a morphism $g: X \rightarrow Y$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ if and only if every lifting problem </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@C =40pt@R=40pt{ A \ar [d]^{f} \ar [r]^-{u_0} &amp;  X \ar [d]^{g} \\ B \ar [r]^-{\overline{u}} \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] &amp;  Y } \]
</div>
<p> admits a <em>unique</em> solution: that is, there is a unique morphism $u: B \rightarrow X$ satisfying $u \circ f = u_0$ and $g \circ u = \overline{u}$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="04NR">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04NR">Remark <span data-tag="04NR">9.6.6.21</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing morphisms $f: A \rightarrow B$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$. Then $f$ is weakly left orthogonal to $g$ if and only if, for every lifting problem $\sigma :$ </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@C =40pt@R=40pt{ A \ar [d]^{f} \ar [r] &amp;  X \ar [d]^{g} \\ B \ar [r] \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] &amp;  Y, } \]
</div>
<p> the solution space $\operatorname{Sol}( \sigma )$ is nonempty. In particular, if $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$, then it is weakly left orthogonal to $g$. Beware that the converse is false in general (Exercise <a data-tag="04NS" href="/tag/04NS">9.6.6.22</a>). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-exercise" id="04NS">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04NS">Exercise <span data-tag="04NS">9.6.6.22</span>.</a> Let $f: A \twoheadrightarrow B$ be a surjective function between sets, and let $g: X \hookrightarrow Y$ be an injective function between sets. Show that: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The morphism $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$ (in the category of sets). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The morphism $g$ is weakly left orthogonal to $f$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Unless either $f$ or $g$ is a bijection, the morphism $g$ is not left orthogonal to $f$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="06WL">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WL">Corollary <span data-tag="06WL">9.6.6.23</span>.</a> Let $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an inner fibration of $\infty $-categories and let $f: A \rightarrow B$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$ be morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$. Assume either that $f$ is $U$-cocartesian or that $g$ is $U$-cartesian. If $U(f)$ is left orthogonal to $U(g)$, then $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Combine Theorem <a data-tag="06WJ" href="/tag/06WJ">9.6.6.19</a> with Corollary <a data-tag="06W0" href="/tag/06W0">9.6.4.23</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="06WM">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WM">Corollary <span data-tag="06WM">9.6.6.24</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing morphisms $f: A \rightarrow B$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$. Then $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$ if and only if the following condition is satisfied: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(\ast )$</dt>
<dd><p>
Suppose we are given a lifting problem 
</p>
<div class="equation" id="06WN">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="06WN" href="/tag/06WN">9.37</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:orthogonal-via-double-slice} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ A \ar [d]^{f} \ar [r] &amp;  X \ar [d]^{g} \\ B \ar [r] \ar@ {--&gt;}[ur] &amp;  Y } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p>
 in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, and let $\widetilde{X}$ and $\widetilde{B}$ be the corresponding objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{A/ \,  /Y}$ (see Remark <a data-tag="04MW" href="/tag/04MW">9.6.4.7</a>). Then the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{A/\, /Y}}( \widetilde{B}, \widetilde{X} )$ is contractible. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Combine Theorem <a data-tag="06WJ" href="/tag/06WJ">9.6.6.19</a> with Proposition <a data-tag="04NV" href="/tag/04NV">9.6.4.21</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="04NY">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04NY">Corollary <span data-tag="04NY">9.6.6.25</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing morphisms $f: A \rightarrow B$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$. Let $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/Y} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ denote the projection map, so that $g$ can be identified with an object $\widetilde{X} \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/Y}$ satisfying $U( \widetilde{X} ) = X$. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The morphism $f$ is left orthogonal to $g$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="04NN" href="/tag/04NN">9.6.6.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every morphism $\widetilde{f}: \widetilde{A} \rightarrow \widetilde{B}$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/Y}$ satisfying $U( \widetilde{f} ) = f$, the object $\widetilde{X} \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/Y}$ is $\widetilde{f}$-local (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="02FZ" href="/tag/02FZ">6.2.3.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Combine Corollary <a data-tag="06WM" href="/tag/06WM">9.6.6.24</a> with Remark <a data-tag="04KJ" href="/tag/04KJ">6.2.3.5</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
As a consequence, we have a partial converse of Corollary <a data-tag="06WD" href="/tag/06WD">9.6.6.15</a>: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="04P8">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04P8">Corollary <span data-tag="04P8">9.6.6.26</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $S$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, and let $g: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which is weakly right orthogonal to $S$. Assume that every morphism $f: A \rightarrow B$ which belongs to $S$ admits a relative codiagonal $\gamma _{A/B}: B \coprod _{A} B \rightarrow B$ which also belongs to $S$ (see Variant <a data-tag="04K7" href="/tag/04K7">7.6.2.23</a>). Then $g$ is right orthogonal to $S$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/Y} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the projection map, and let us identify $g$ with an object $\widetilde{X} \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/Y}$ satisfying $U( \widetilde{X} ) = X$. Let $\widetilde{S}$ denote the collection of those morphisms $\widetilde{f}$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/Y}$ which satisfy $U( \widetilde{f} ) \in S$. Our assumption that $g$ is weakly right orthogonal to $S$ guarantees that $\widetilde{X}$ is weakly $\widetilde{S}$-local (Remark <a data-tag="04N2" href="/tag/04N2">9.6.4.12</a>). It follows from Proposition <a data-tag="02KB" href="/tag/02KB">7.1.4.26</a> that every morphism of $\widetilde{S}$ admits a relative codiagonal which also belongs to $\widetilde{S}$, so that $\widetilde{X}$ is $\widetilde{S}$-local (Proposition <a data-tag="04LY" href="/tag/04LY">9.6.2.14</a>). Invoking Corollary <a data-tag="04NY" href="/tag/04NY">9.6.6.25</a>, we conclude that $g$ is right orthogonal to $S$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
We conclude this section with a proof of Proposition <a data-tag="06WG" href="/tag/06WG">9.6.6.18</a>. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-notation" id="06WP">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WP">Notation <span data-tag="06WP">9.6.6.27</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category equipped with diagrams $F: J \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $G: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Constructions <a data-tag="04J1" href="/tag/04J1">4.6.6.4</a> and <a data-tag="03JY" href="/tag/03JY">8.1.2.7</a> then supply comparison maps </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \{  F \}  \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}(J, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) } \operatorname{Fun}(J \star K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \leftarrow \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{F/} ) \quad \quad \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{F/} \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} }( J^{\operatorname{op}}, \operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})) \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}(J^{\operatorname{op}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) } \operatorname{\mathcal{C}} \]
</div>
<p> which are equivalences of $\infty $-categories (Theorem <a data-tag="04J2" href="/tag/04J2">4.6.6.5</a> and Corollary <a data-tag="06TQ" href="/tag/06TQ">8.1.2.21</a>). Applying the construction $\operatorname{Fun}(K, \bullet )$ on the right hand side and passing to fibers over $G \in \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$, we obtain a diagram of Kan complexes </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \{  F \}  \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}(J, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) } \operatorname{Fun}(J \star K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) } \{ G\}  \leftarrow \operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{F/} ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \times \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( J^{\operatorname{op}} \times K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \]
</div>
<p> where both maps are homotopy equivalences (Corollary <a data-tag="01HK" href="/tag/01HK">4.5.3.31</a> and Remark <a data-tag="01HA" href="/tag/01HA">4.5.1.16</a>). Note that this diagram depends functorially on $J$ and $K$ (as objects of the category $(\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }})_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$). </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof of Proposition <a data-tag="06WG" href="/tag/06WG">9.6.6.18</a>.</strong>
       Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $f: A \rightarrow B$ and $g: X \rightarrow Y$ be morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Set $J = \Delta ^1 = K$, so that $f$ and $g$ can be identified with diagrams $F: J \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $G: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, respectively. The diagram 
    <p>
<div class="equation" id="06WQ">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="06WQ" href="/tag/06WQ">9.38</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:concrete-model-for-Hom} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(B,X) \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X) \ar [d] \\ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( B, Y) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A, Y) } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p>
       appearing in Definition <a data-tag="04NN" href="/tag/04NN">9.6.6.1</a> is characterized, up to isomorphism in $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{S}})$, by the requirement that it is a covariant transport representation for the left fibration 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  U: J^{\operatorname{op}} \times _{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} } \operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \times _{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} K \rightarrow J^{\operatorname{op}} \times K. \]
</div>
<p>
       Since $J^{\operatorname{op}} \times K$ is (the nerve of) an ordinary category, we can identify (<a data-tag="06WQ" href="/tag/06WQ">9.38</a>) with the (strictly) commutative diagram of Kan complexes obtained from the strict transport representation of Construction <a data-tag="035J" href="/tag/035J">5.3.1.5</a> (Example <a data-tag="0386" href="/tag/0386">5.6.5.7</a>). Setting $J_0 = \{ 0\}  \subseteq J$ and $K_0 = \{ 1\}  \subseteq K$, this diagram is given by 
    <p>
<div class="equation" id="06WR">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="06WR" href="/tag/06WR">9.39</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:concrete-model-for-Hom2} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \times \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( J^{\operatorname{op}} \times K, \operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) ) \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \times \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( J^{\operatorname{op}}_{0} \times K, \operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) ) \ar [d] \\ \operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \times \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( J^{\operatorname{op}} \times K_0, \operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) ) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \times \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( J^{\operatorname{op}}_0 \times K_0, \operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) ).} \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p>
       Applying Notation <a data-tag="06WP" href="/tag/06WP">9.6.6.27</a>, we obtain an equivalence to (<a data-tag="06WR" href="/tag/06WR">9.39</a>) from the diagram of Kan complexes 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Fun}_{/\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/} ) \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{A/} ) \ar [d] \\ \operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( K_0, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{f/} ) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Fun}_{ / \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( K_0, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{A/} ), } \]
</div>
<p>
       which in turn admits an equivalence to the diagram 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,X,Y)_{g} \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,X,Y)_{g} \ar [d] \\ _{f}\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,B,Y) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(A,Y) } \]
</div>
<p>
       appearing in the statement of Proposition <a data-tag="06WG" href="/tag/06WG">9.6.6.18</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></article>
